doc_id: fastapi-settings-patterns
title: FastAPI Settings Integration Patterns
project: ai-workflow
version_hint: "2024-2025"
kind: fastapi_integration_standards
description: FastAPI-specific patterns for integrating Settings with dependency injection, application factory, and app.state. Covers get_settings dependency, @lru_cache usage, and runtime access patterns.
scope: Covers Settings DI integration, factory wiring, app.state storage, dependency injection patterns, and testing with overrides. Python/Pydantic validation patterns are in docs/development/python/settings-patterns.toon.
primary_runtime_references[2]:
  - path: app/core/dependencies.py
    role: get_settings dependency exposing the Settings model.
  - path: app/core/factory.py
    role: create_app factory that accepts and stores Settings.
related_pattern_docs[5]:
  - python/settings-patterns.toon
  - factory-patterns.toon
  - python/dependency-patterns.toon
  - fastapi/dependency-patterns.toon
  - service-patterns.toon
sections[5]:
  - id: get-settings-dependency
    index: 1
    title: get_settings Dependency
    category: standard
    summary: Patterns for exposing Settings via FastAPI dependency injection using @lru_cache.
    sample_code:
      description: Implementation of get_settings with @lru_cache for singleton Settings access.
      imports[2]: functools.lru_cache,app.core.settings.Settings
      code: |
        from functools import lru_cache
        from app.core.settings import Settings
        @lru_cache(maxsize=1)
        def get_settings() -> Settings:
            """Return cached Settings."""
            return Settings()
    items[5]:
      - id: get-settings.lru-cache
        type: rule
        text: Implement get_settings in app/core/dependencies.py as an @lru_cache-decorated function that builds a single Settings instance from environment variables.
      - id: get-settings.singleton-behavior
        type: note
        text: The @lru_cache decorator ensures get_settings() returns the same Settings instance across all calls, avoiding repeated environment variable parsing and validation.
      - id: get-settings.maxsize-one
        type: rule
        text: Use maxsize=1 for @lru_cache since only one Settings instance is needed per process lifecycle.
      - id: get-settings.module-location
        type: rule
        text: Place get_settings in app/core/dependencies.py so it is framework-agnostic and can be used by both FastAPI routes and non-HTTP code paths.
      - id: get-settings.no-direct-construction
        type: rule
        text: Avoid constructing Settings() directly in route handlers or services; always use get_settings dependency or receive Settings via constructor injection.
  - id: app-state-storage
    index: 2
    title: Settings Storage on app.state
    category: standard
    summary: Patterns for storing and accessing Settings via app.state in the application factory.
    sample_code:
      description: Storing Settings on app.state in create_app.
      imports[3]: fastapi.FastAPI,fastapi.responses.ORJSONResponse,app.core.settings.Settings
      code: |
        from fastapi import FastAPI
        from fastapi.responses import ORJSONResponse
        from app.core.settings import Settings
        def create_app(settings: Settings) -> FastAPI:
            app = FastAPI(
                title=settings.app_name,
                version=settings.app_version,
                default_response_class=ORJSONResponse,
            )
            app.state.settings = settings
            # ... router registration, middleware configuration ...
            return app
    items[5]:
      - id: app-state.store-on-create
        type: rule
        text: Store the Settings instance on app.state.settings immediately after FastAPI app creation in create_app so components with Request or App access can read configuration.
      - id: app-state.factory-entrypoint
        type: note
        text: Treat create_app(settings: Settings) as the canonical composition root; production startup and tests should both construct apps by passing in validated Settings instances.
      - id: app-state.middleware-access
        type: pattern
        text: Middleware and exception handlers that need Settings access can retrieve it from request.app.state.settings when they do not have access to dependency injection.
      - id: app-state.single-instance
        type: rule
        text: Avoid constructing additional Settings instances at runtime; tests may create extra instances explicitly, but the running app should rely on a single shared instance.
      - id: app-state.no-direct-modification
        type: rule
        text: Do not modify app.state.settings after application startup; treat the Settings instance as immutable once stored.
  - id: route-dependency-injection
    index: 3
    title: Route Dependency Injection
    category: standard
    summary: Patterns for injecting Settings into FastAPI route handlers using Depends().
    sample_code:
      description: Injecting Settings into route handlers using Annotated type aliases.
      imports[4]: typing.Annotated,fastapi.Depends,fastapi.APIRouter,app.core.settings.Settings
      code: |
        from typing import Annotated
        from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends
        from app.core.dependencies import get_settings
        from app.core.settings import Settings
        SettingsDep = Annotated[Settings, Depends(get_settings)]
        router = APIRouter()
        @router.get("/config")
        async def get_config(settings: SettingsDep) -> dict[str, str]:
            return {"app_name": settings.app_name, "version": settings.app_version}
    items[5]:
      - id: route-di.annotated-alias
        type: pattern
        text: Define a SettingsDep type alias as Annotated[Settings, Depends(get_settings)] and use it in route signatures for concise and consistent injection.
      - id: route-di.centralized-aliases
        type: rule
        text: Place the SettingsDep alias in app/api/v1/dependencies.py alongside other dependency type aliases so routers import from a single versioned module.
      - id: route-di.route-usage
        type: pattern
        text: Inject Settings into routes using settings: SettingsDep rather than calling get_settings() directly inside the handler body.
      - id: route-di.pass-to-services
        type: note
        text: Routes that need to pass Settings to services should inject it via the dependency system and pass it as a constructor argument to service factories.
      - id: route-di.no-globals
        type: rule
        text: Avoid importing or using a global Settings instance in routes; always rely on dependency injection for testability and explicit configuration.
  - id: service-integration
    index: 4
    title: Service and Infrastructure Integration
    category: standard
    summary: Patterns for passing Settings to services and infrastructure components.
    items[5]:
      - id: service-integration.constructor-injection
        type: rule
        text: Services receive Settings via constructor injection; define service factory dependencies that accept SettingsDep and pass it to the service constructor.
      - id: service-integration.infrastructure-factories
        type: pattern
        text: Connection factories such as create_surrealdb_pool and create_elasticsearch_wrapper receive Settings instances and read only the fields they need.
      - id: service-integration.lifespan-usage
        type: note
        text: The lifespan context manager in app/core/factory.py receives Settings via the closure over create_app parameters and uses it to initialize pools and clients.
      - id: service-integration.no-settings-in-services
        type: rule
        text: Services should not import get_settings directly; they should receive Settings via constructor parameters, keeping them framework-agnostic.
      - id: service-integration.ref-to-service-patterns
        type: note
        text: For additional patterns on services that consume settings, see docs/development/service-patterns.toon.
  - id: testing-with-overrides
    index: 5
    title: Testing with Dependency Overrides
    category: testing
    summary: Patterns for overriding get_settings in tests to supply test-specific configuration.
    sample_code:
      description: Overriding get_settings in tests using app.dependency_overrides.
      imports[4]: pytest,fastapi.FastAPI,app.core.settings.Settings,app.core.dependencies.get_settings
      code: |
        import pytest
        from fastapi import FastAPI
        from app.core.factory import create_app
        from app.core.settings import Settings
        from app.core.dependencies import get_settings
        @pytest.fixture
        def test_settings() -> Settings:
            return Settings()
        @pytest.fixture
        def test_app(test_settings: Settings) -> FastAPI:
            app = create_app(test_settings)
            app.dependency_overrides[get_settings] = lambda: test_settings
            return app
    items[6]:
      - id: testing.factory-app
        type: rule
        text: Construct FastAPI apps in tests using create_app with explicit Settings instances instead of importing an app from app.main.
      - id: testing.override-get-settings
        type: rule
        text: Override get_settings via app.dependency_overrides[get_settings] to supply test-specific configuration.
      - id: testing.clear-overrides
        type: note
        text: Clear app.dependency_overrides after each test or use fresh app instances per test to avoid state leakage.
      - id: testing.model-copy-updates
        type: pattern
        text: When tests need custom configuration, start from test_settings and use model_copy(update={...}) to override specific fields without reconstructing the entire Settings.
      - id: testing.fixture-composition
        type: note
        text: Compose test fixtures such as test_settings → test_app → async_client so overrides propagate consistently through the test infrastructure.
      - id: testing.no-lru-cache-clear
        type: rule
        text: Do not rely on clearing @lru_cache in tests; use dependency overrides instead, which is the FastAPI-native pattern for test isolation.
